// Generated by CRM App/sync/build_scripts.py from the vault call scripts. Don't edit by hand.
window.AWD_SCRIPTS = {"A": "<p><i>v4, 2026-10-10: based on the cold-call script Johnny shared (get in conversation → offer to build something → book a 10-minute call). Adapted to AWD's real prices and kept truthful. Caller: Johnny. For businesses with <b>no website</b> (or only Facebook / Google). Older versions: Old Scripts/Call Script - List A (No Website) v2.3 and v3 in Git/vault history.</i></p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> first, just <b>get in conversation</b> (not getting hung up on is a win). Best case: <b>book a 10-minute call</b> (today or tomorrow) to show your work and give them a free look at their online presence. A warm lead you can call back is also a win.</p>\n<p><b>Before you dial (30 seconds):</b> owner's name (check their Google reviews, owners often reply to reviews), their niche (\"the plumber\", \"the hair salon\"), and Abby's <b>Opener</b>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3>1. Confirm who you've got</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, is this <b>[name / business name]</b>?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"Yeah, it is.\"</i></p>\n<h3>2. The opener (pick ONE)</h3>\n<p><b>Default (always true):</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, what's up <b>[name]</b>! I came across y'all on Google, and it looks like y'all do super high-quality work. <b>[Abby's opener, e.g. \"47 five-star reviews, that's no joke.\"]</b> Y'all are <b>the [niche]</b>, right?\" <i>(e.g. \"the handyman, right?\", \"the hair salon, right?\")</i></blockquote>\n<p><b>Only if someone actually referred this lead to you</b> (use their real name):</p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, what's up <b>[name]</b>! <b>[Referrer]</b> referred me to you guys, said y'all do super high-quality work. Y'all are <b>the [niche]</b>, right?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"Yeah, that's right.\"</i></p>\n<h3>3. Get them talking</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Sweet! Yeah, I was looking y'all up and it looks like y'all don't have a website, just <b>[a Google page / Facebook]</b>?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Either they agree, or they say \"we've got a Google page\" → \"But no website, right?\" Either way you're in conversation, which is the most important thing.</i></p>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"Yeah, we don't have a website.\"</i></p>\n<h3>4. Who you are + the offer</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Okay, cool. Well, I don't want to waste your time. I just graduated college, and I've started a little web design business here in Bay Area Houston. Right now I'm building websites <b>super cheap</b>, hoping I can get a testimonial from you saying I did a good job, I was fast, you saw results, that kind of thing.<br>Do you mind if I build something out for you to take a look at?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>This is where objections start. See section 8.</i></p>\n<h3>5. If they ask \"How much?\"</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"So it's <b>$200</b> and the website's yours. If you want me to handle the hosting, your web address and changes for you month to month, it's <b>$50 a month</b>. If not, no big deal, 200 and it's yours. <i>(+$25 if I set up the web address for you.)</i><br>If you've got like <b>10 minutes today or tomorrow</b>, I can walk you through some of the work I've done and give you a little free look at how y'all show up online.\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Optional, depending on the flow:</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"All I'd need from you is a quick form with some basic info about your business. Takes two minutes.\" <i>(Text them: affordablewebdesigns.vercel.app/?src=call#contact)</i></blockquote>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"Okay, yeah, why not. Call me back later today or tomorrow and we can set something up.\"</i></p>\n<h3>6. Lock the appointment (don't settle for \"call me back\")</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Sweet! Even better than a call back, let me just put it on my calendar, give me one sec. How's <b>[time] today</b>? I've also got <b>[time] tomorrow</b>. What works better for you?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Only two options. Don't give them too many.</i></p>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"[Time] should work.\"</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Perfect. Besides the off chance there's a zombie apocalypse at <b>[time]</b>, is there any reason you wouldn't be able to make it?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>(Get as much commitment as you can, so they actually show up.)</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Sweet. At the very least you'll learn a little about how your business looks online from a customer's point of view, plus see what some of your competitors are doing to market themselves.\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>(Give them a reason to show up. It shouldn't sound like a sales call.)</i></p>\n<h3>7. Close the call</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Awesome, appreciate your time, <b>[name]</b>. I'll talk to you <b>[day/time]</b>. Have a good one, man!\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>In the CRM:</b> call status <b>Follow Up</b>, follow-up date = the call day, note the time. Ask Claude to have Dex build their homepage before the call so you have something to show.</p>\n<h3>8. Objection handling</h3>\n<p><i>Always agree with and understand the objection first. Don't sound like you're trying to convince them.</i></p>\n<p><b>\"We don't need a website. We already have enough business.\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"I totally understand, I hear that all the time. The reason a website still works is it lets you charge more for the same work you're already doing. If you're already turning work away, you can quote the overflow at a higher price and see if they say yes. If they don't, you're right where you were. If they do, you just made money you wouldn't have, doing the exact same job.\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>\"It seems too expensive.\"</b> <i>(easiest one)</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Totally fair. Let me ask you, what's a typical job worth for y'all?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Then do the simple math with their number. E.g. a $500 job: the site is $200 once, or $50 a month. One extra customer a year pays for it several times over.</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"If I can't get you at least one extra job a year with this, I'd probably just go play in traffic.\" <i>(Say it as a joke. The point is it's hard not to get a return.)</i></blockquote>\n<p><b>\"Just send me something.\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Even better, I'll build it and text you the link. What's the best number? And let's grab 10 minutes so I can walk you through it.\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>\"I'm not interested.\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"No worries at all. Mind if I check back in a little while?\" <i>(Log it. If they say don't call again, log <b>Asked not to call</b>; the CRM disqualifies them automatically.)</i></blockquote>\n<p><b>\"Who are you again? / Is this a sales call?\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Ha, fair question. I'm Johnny, I'm local, I just started building websites for small businesses here. No pressure at all, I just want to show you something.\"</blockquote>\n<h3>9. If someone else answers</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, this is Johnny, I'm a local web designer. I'm trying to reach <b>[owner]</b> about the business's website. Is he/she around?\"</blockquote>\n<ul class=\"dot\">\n<li><b>Not in:</b> \"No problem. When's a good time to catch him/her? Is there a better number?\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>10. Voicemail (under 20 seconds)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey <b>[name]</b>, this is Johnny, a local web designer. I came across <b>[business]</b> on Google, y'all have great reviews, but I didn't see a website. I'd love to build y'all one super cheap and show it to you. Call or text me at <b>832-508-0059</b>. Again, 832-508-0059.\"</blockquote>\n<h3>11. Text after a voicemail or no answer</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey [name], it's Johnny, local web designer. Just left you a voicemail. I'd love to build [business] a website and show it to you on a quick 10-min call. 832-508-0059\"</blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3>Mindset</h3>\n<p>1. <b>The first goal is just to not get hung up on.</b> Getting in conversation on a cold call is a win. Booking a call is the best case, and a warm lead is also a win.</p>\n<p>2. <b>Don't sound like a telemarketer.</b> Crack a joke, be a little bold. You want them thinking \"this guy seems cool and real, not like the other cold calls I get.\" Reading off a script like a robot gets you hung up on every time.</p>\n<p>3. <b>The money is in the follow-up.</b> Most closes happen after <b>5 to 11 calls</b>. If they've shown interest, keep calling back until they tell you they're not interested. Get used to rejection. Embrace it.</p>\n<p><b>Rules:</b> dial by hand, never promise anything not in Offer &amp; Pricing. Use the referral opener only for real referrals. Respect \"don't call me\" right away. Log every call in the CRM.</p>", "B": "<p><i>v4, 2026-10-10: based on the cold-call script Johnny shared (get in conversation → offer to build something → book a 10-minute call). Adapted to AWD's real prices and kept truthful. Caller: Johnny. For businesses that <b>already have a website or own a domain</b>. Same flow as Call Script - List A (No Website). Older versions: Old Scripts/Call Script - List B (Has Website) v2.3 and v3 in Git/vault history.</i></p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> first, just <b>get in conversation</b> (not getting hung up on is a win). Best case: <b>book a 10-minute call</b> (today or tomorrow) to show your work and give them a free look at their online presence. A warm lead you can call back is also a win.</p>\n<p><b>Before you dial (1 minute):</b> open their site <b>on your phone</b> and note one specific, fair problem from Abby's <b>Website notes</b> (small text, no tap-to-call, looks dated, slow). Plus the owner's name (check their Google reviews), their niche, and Abby's <b>Opener</b>. Never trash their site.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3>1. Confirm who you've got</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, is this <b>[name / business name]</b>?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"Yeah, it is.\"</i></p>\n<h3>2. The opener (pick ONE)</h3>\n<p><b>Default (always true):</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, what's up <b>[name]</b>! I came across y'all on Google, and it looks like y'all do super high-quality work. <b>[Abby's opener, e.g. \"47 five-star reviews, that's no joke.\"]</b> Y'all are <b>the [niche]</b>, right?\" <i>(e.g. \"the handyman, right?\", \"the hair salon, right?\")</i></blockquote>\n<p><b>Only if someone actually referred this lead to you</b> (use their real name):</p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, what's up <b>[name]</b>! <b>[Referrer]</b> referred me to you guys, said y'all do super high-quality work. Y'all are <b>the [niche]</b>, right?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"Yeah, that's right.\"</i></p>\n<h3>3. Get them talking</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Sweet! Yeah, I pulled up y'all's website on my phone. Y'all have good info on there, but <b>[one specific, fair issue: the text is tiny on a phone / there's no button to tap and call / it looks like it hasn't been updated in a while]</b>. Who put that together for y'all?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Let them talk. Agree first. You're in conversation, which is the most important thing.</i></p>\n<h3>4. Who you are + the offer</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Okay, cool. Well, I don't want to waste your time. I just graduated college, and I've started a little web design business here in Bay Area Houston. Right now I'm building websites <b>super cheap</b>, hoping I can get a testimonial from you saying I did a good job, I was fast, you saw results, that kind of thing.<br>Do you mind if I build a fresh version of your site for you to take a look at, side by side with the one you've got?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>This is where objections start. See section 8.</i></p>\n<h3>5. If they ask \"How much?\"</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"So it's <b>$200</b> to switch and the website's yours. You keep your same web address and email. If you want me to handle the hosting and changes for you month to month, it's <b>$50 a month</b>. If not, no big deal, 200 and it's yours.<br>If you've got like <b>10 minutes today or tomorrow</b>, I can walk you through some of the work I've done and give you a little free look at how y'all show up online.\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Optional, depending on the flow:</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"All I'd need from you is a quick form with some basic info about your business. Takes two minutes.\" <i>(Text them: affordablewebdesigns.vercel.app/?src=call#contact)</i></blockquote>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"Okay, yeah, why not. Call me back later today or tomorrow and we can set something up.\"</i></p>\n<h3>6. Lock the appointment (don't settle for \"call me back\")</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Sweet! Even better than a call back, let me just put it on my calendar, give me one sec. How's <b>[time] today</b>? I've also got <b>[time] tomorrow</b>. What works better for you?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Only two options. Don't give them too many.</i></p>\n<p><i>Prospect: \"[Time] should work.\"</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Perfect. Besides the off chance there's a zombie apocalypse at <b>[time]</b>, is there any reason you wouldn't be able to make it?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>(Get as much commitment as you can, so they actually show up.)</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Sweet. At the very least you'll learn a little about how your business looks online from a customer's point of view, plus see what some of your competitors are doing to market themselves.\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>(Give them a reason to show up. It shouldn't sound like a sales call.)</i></p>\n<h3>7. Close the call</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Awesome, appreciate your time, <b>[name]</b>. I'll talk to you <b>[day/time]</b>. Have a good one, man!\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>In the CRM:</b> call status <b>Follow Up</b>, follow-up date = the call day, note the time. Ask Claude to have Dex build their homepage before the call so you have something to show.</p>\n<h3>8. Objection handling</h3>\n<p><i>Always agree with and understand the objection first. Don't sound like you're trying to convince them.</i></p>\n<p><b>\"We already have a website.\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Totally, and I'm not saying get rid of it. I'll build a fresh version side by side. If yours is better, you've lost nothing.\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>\"What happens to my domain and email?\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"You keep your same web address, in your name, and your email stays exactly how it is. I just point the address at the new site.\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>\"We don't need a better website. We already have enough business.\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"I totally understand, I hear that all the time. The reason a website still works is it lets you charge more for the same work you're already doing. If you're already turning work away, you can quote the overflow at a higher price and see if they say yes. If they don't, you're right where you were. If they do, you just made money you wouldn't have, doing the exact same job.\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>\"It seems too expensive.\"</b> <i>(easiest one)</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Totally fair. Let me ask you, what's a typical job worth for y'all?\"</blockquote>\n<p><i>Then do the simple math with their number. E.g. a $500 job: the site is $200 once, or $50 a month. One extra customer a year pays for it several times over.</i></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"If I can't get you at least one extra job a year with this, I'd probably just go play in traffic.\" <i>(Say it as a joke. The point is it's hard not to get a return.)</i></blockquote>\n<p><b>\"Just send me something.\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Even better, I'll build it and text you the link. What's the best number? And let's grab 10 minutes so I can walk you through it.\"</blockquote>\n<p><b>\"I'm not interested.\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"No worries at all. Mind if I check back in a little while?\" <i>(Log it. If they say don't call again, log <b>Asked not to call</b>; the CRM disqualifies them automatically.)</i></blockquote>\n<p><b>\"Who are you again? / Is this a sales call?\"</b></p>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Ha, fair question. I'm Johnny, I'm local, I just started building websites for small businesses here. No pressure at all, I just want to show you something.\"</blockquote>\n<h3>9. If someone else answers</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey, this is Johnny, I'm a local web designer. I'm trying to reach <b>[owner]</b> about the business's website. Is he/she around?\"</blockquote>\n<ul class=\"dot\">\n<li><b>Not in:</b> \"No problem. When's a good time to catch him/her? Is there a better number?\"</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>10. Voicemail (under 20 seconds)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey <b>[name]</b>, this is Johnny, a local web designer. I came across <b>[business]</b> on Google, y'all have great reviews. I'd love to build y'all a fresh version of your website super cheap and show it to you side by side. Call or text me at <b>832-508-0059</b>. Again, 832-508-0059.\"</blockquote>\n<h3>11. Text after a voicemail or no answer</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"say\">\"Hey [name], it's Johnny, local web designer. Just left you a voicemail. I'd love to build [business] a fresh version of your website and show it to you side by side on a quick 10-min call. 832-508-0059\"</blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3>Mindset</h3>\n<p>1. <b>The first goal is just to not get hung up on.</b> Getting in conversation on a cold call is a win. Booking a call is the best case, and a warm lead is also a win.</p>\n<p>2. <b>Don't sound like a telemarketer.</b> Crack a joke, be a little bold. You want them thinking \"this guy seems cool and real, not like the other cold calls I get.\" Reading off a script like a robot gets you hung up on every time.</p>\n<p>3. <b>The money is in the follow-up.</b> Most closes happen after <b>5 to 11 calls</b>. If they've shown interest, keep calling back until they tell you they're not interested. Get used to rejection. Embrace it.</p>\n<p><b>Rules:</b> dial by hand, never promise anything not in Offer &amp; Pricing. Use the referral opener only for real referrals. Respect \"don't call me\" right away. Log every call in the CRM.</p>"};
